  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nLate morning water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal a fairly flat ridge centered over the southern \nplains, allowing modest westerly flow across the central Rockies. \nAdditionally, a shortwave trough was noted over western WY moving \neast-southeast. At the surface, broad high pressure is centered over \nthe Midwest, leading to southeasterly winds across southwest KS. \nDespite the marginally weaker ridge aloft, models show 850-mb \ntemperatures will rise by around 3-5 degrees C, which will translate \nto afternoon highs warming into the upper 90s to low 100 for most, \nwhile the northern zones stay a bit cooler in the lower 90s courtesy \nof lingering cloud cover this morning. A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect for Meade and Clark counties where dewpoints in the upper \n50s/low 60s combine with temperatures in the low 100s to support \nheat indices at or above 105 degrees F. While adjacent counties will \nlikely fail to reach Heat Advisory criteria, precautions should \nstill be taken to limit outside exposure.\n\nFocus then shifts to the first opportunity for severe thunderstorms \nin our area in quite some time. As the aforementioned shortwave \ntrough over western WY approaches later this afternoon, HREF members \nall agree scattered thunderstorms will develop across southwest KS \nby 21-22Z. With gusty surface southeasterlies beneath ~20 kt \nwesterly flow at 500-mb, and strong surface heating amidst upper 50s \nto mid 60s dewpoints, the CAPE/shear parameter space will be \nsufficiently supportive of organized convection. HREF members \nsuggest the primary storm mode will be a combination of high-based \nsupercells and bowing segments that will pose a mainly damaging wind \ngust risk, although isolated large hail will also be possible. This \nactivity should weaken quickly after sunset as the boundary layer \nstabilizes, but convection will likely linger over our eastern zones \nthrough sunrise Friday morning.\n\nDaytime Friday, short and medium range ensembles agree the upper \nlevel ridge will begin to rebuild northwestward to near the Four \nCorners region, and this ridge will only propagate slightly eastward \nthrough the rest of the forecast period. This synoptic evolution \nwill result in a resumption of the dry conditions across the central \nplains, along with daily afternoon temperatures in the upper 90s to \nlow 100s. At the moment, Monday and Tuesday look particularly hot as \nhighs in the 105-110 range appear likely for much of the area. \nTherefore, multiple days of Heat Advisories, and perhaps some \nExcessive Heat Warnings, will assuredly be forthcoming.",
